{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-post-tsx","path":"/blog/2025-10-15--systematic-testing-of-c-abstraction-recovery-systems-by-iterative-compiler-model-refinement-published-at-sure-2025/","result":{"data":{"site":{"siteMetadata":{"disqus":"edward-j-schwartz"}},"post":{"htmlAst":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"h2","properties":{"id":"systematic-testing-of-c-abstraction-recovery-systems-by-iterative-compiler-model-refinement","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#systematic-testing-of-c-abstraction-recovery-systems-by-iterative-compiler-model-refinement","ariaLabel":"systematic testing of c abstraction recovery systems by iterative compiler model refinement permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"text","value":"Systematic Testing of C++ Abstraction Recovery Systems by Iterative Compiler Model Refinement"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I'm excited to announce that our paper, "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://edmcman.github.io/papers/sure2025.pdf"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Systematic Testing of C++ Abstraction\nRecovery Systems by Iterative Compiler Model\nRefinement\""}]},{"type":"text","value":", has been published\nat the "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://sure-workshop.org/"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2025 Workshop on Software Understanding and Reverse Engineering\n(SURE)"}]},{"type":"text","value":"! SURE is a new workshop—more details\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#sure"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"below"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When I joined SEI, one of my first research projects was improving C++\nabstraction recovery from binaries. This work led to the development of\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://edmcman.github.io/papers/ccs18.pdf"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"OOAnalyzer"}]},{"type":"text","value":", a tool that combines\npractical reverse engineering with academic techniques like a Prolog-based\nreasoning engine."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Over time, users increasingly adopted OOAnalyze and ran it on video game\nexecutables that were much larger and more complex than the malware samples we\nhad originally targeted.  Perhaps unsurprisingly, running OOAnalyzer on these\nlarger, more complex binaries often revealed problematic corner cases in\nOOAnalyzer's rules.  As we learned about such problems, we would attempt to\nimprove the rules to handle the new cases.  Sometimes this was pretty easy; some\nof the problems were obvious in hindsight.  But not always."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eventually, I found that some rules were becoming so nuanced and\ncomplex that I was having trouble reasoning about them.  I realized that I\nneeded a more systematic way to test and refine OOAnalyzer's rules.  This\nrealization led to the work presented in this paper."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"On one hand, I'm incredibly proud of this paper.  To greatly simplify, we\ndeveloped a way to model check the rules in OOAnalyzer.  It's been incredibly\neffective at finding problems in OOAnalyzer's rules.  We found 27 soundness\nissues in OOAnalyzer, and two in VirtAnalyzer, a competing C++ abstraction\nrecovery system."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unfortunately, the journey to publication for this paper has been long and\nbumpy.  It is, admittedly, a fairly niche topic.  But I also think it has some\ninteresting ideas that could be applied more generally to other reverse\nengineering systems.  In particular, I think that much of what we call \"binary\nanalysis\" is actually \"compiled executable analysis\" in disguise.  In other\nwords, our binary analyses are often making (substantial!) assumptions about how\ncompilers work, such as the calling conventions that are used, and without these\nassumptions they "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"do not work"}]},{"type":"text","value":".  Our paper provides a systematic way to encode\nand refine these assumptions, and validate whether underlying analyses are\ncorrect under the assumptions.  My hope is that in future work, we are able to\ndemonstrate how to apply this overall approach to more traditional binary\nanalyses. I personally think that static binary analysis "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"without"}]},{"type":"text","value":" such\nreasonable simplifying assumptions is impractical, so we need more techniques\nlike this to help analyze compiled executables."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Although I'm proud of the problems we discovered in OOAnalyzer, I'm also a bit\ndisappointed in the practical impact. My hope was that when we found and fixed\nsoundness problems in OOAnalyzer's rules, it would generally improve the\nperformance of OOAnalyzer on real-world binaries.  However, we found that in\ngeneral this was not true.  In fact, in some cases, fixing soundness problems\nactually made OOAnalyzer perform worse on real-world binaries!  I think this\nhighlights that there is a trade-off between soundness and accuracy in binary\nanalysis.  In order to be sound, the system can "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"never"}]},{"type":"text","value":" make mistakes.  We may\nneed to make a rule more conservative, even if it almost never poses a problem\nin practice. However, in order to maximize accuracy, the system must optimize\nfor the most common cases, even if it means that it will occasionally make\nmistakes."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"h2","properties":{"id":"a-namesureasure-workshop","style":"position:relative;"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"#a-namesureasure-workshop","ariaLabel":"a namesureasure workshop permalink","className":["anchor","before"]},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"svg","properties":{"ariaHidden":"true","focusable":"false","height":"16","version":"1.1","viewBox":"0 0 16 16","width":"16"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"path","properties":{"fillRule":"evenodd","d":"M4 9h1v1H4c-1.5 0-3-1.69-3-3.5S2.55 3 4 3h4c1.45 0 3 1.69 3 3.5 0 1.41-.91 2.72-2 3.25V8.59c.58-.45 1-1.27 1-2.09C10 5.22 8.98 4 8 4H4c-.98 0-2 1.22-2 2.5S3 9 4 9zm9-3h-1v1h1c1 0 2 1.22 2 2.5S13.98 12 13 12H9c-.98 0-2-1.22-2-2.5 0-.83.42-1.64 1-2.09V6.25c-1.09.53-2 1.84-2 3.25C6 11.31 7.55 13 9 13h4c1.45 0 3-1.69 3-3.5S14.5 6 13 6z"},"children":[]}]}]},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"name":"sure"},"children":[]},{"type":"text","value":"SURE Workshop"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"SURE, the Workshop on Software Understanding and Reverse Engineering, is a new\nworkshop colocated with ACM CCS this year. In addition to presenting our paper,\nI had the honor of participating in a panel on \"Modern Software Understanding\"\nalongside Fish Wang and Steffen Enders. The first SURE was a resounding success,\nand I'm eager to see how it evolves in the coming years!"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[]},{"type":"element","tagName":"figure","properties":{"className":["gatsby-resp-image-figure"],"style":""},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"\n    "},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"className":["gatsby-resp-image-wrapper"],"style":"position: relative; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; max-width: 1080px; "},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"\n      "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"className":["gatsby-resp-image-link"],"href":"/static/7318a7a5017da30dd4f36cf85e46c2cd/302a4/ed-panel.png","style":"display: block","target":"_blank","rel":["noopener"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"\n    "},{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"className":["gatsby-resp-image-background-image"],"style":"padding-bottom: 133.33333333333331%; position: relative; bottom: 0; left: 0; background-image: url('data:image/png;base64,'); background-size: cover; display: block;"},"children":[]},{"type":"text","value":"\n  "},{"type":"element","tagName":"picture","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"\n          "},{"type":"element","tagName":"source","properties":{"srcSet":["/static/7318a7a5017da30dd4f36cf85e46c2cd/e46ac/ed-panel.avif 450w","/static/7318a7a5017da30dd4f36cf85e46c2cd/9b8d4/ed-panel.avif 900w","/static/7318a7a5017da30dd4f36cf85e46c2cd/d8ca6/ed-panel.avif 1080w"],"sizes":"(max-width: 1080px) 100vw, 1080px","type":"image/avif"},"children":[]},{"